e, written by the one writer in the same call as the requested ids on either route, carried unchanged by the outcome rewrite as the identity cell is; a brief naming none binds none, read as absent -> not the same: a new binding cell and its lifecycle. Ready to bless. It keeps the one-writer rule, and it states the absent case that LLR-069 refuses on, so the producer and the consumer agree.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] LLR-314 Detail -> before: the dispute grammar's findings-file reader, request line and ruling parser -> after: the same, plus the composed brief's findings-file line, whose first occurrence supplies the path, with none or an empty one supplying none -> not the same: a new grammar line. Ready to bless. It is closed (first line wins, and an empty line supplies nothing), and TC-334's new clause and case hold it.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] LLR-315 Detail -> before: `dispute_values` fills the request with the requested ids -> after: the request also carries, after the ids, the findings-file line naming the one reference the assembler read -> not the same: the composed brief carries a new line. Ready to bless. It ties the record to the file actually read, which is the Trust section's producer rule (the binding records the path `dispute_values` read), and it adds no name: the function was named in the approved cell already.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] TC-334 Expected, Method -> before: the dispute brief, grammar, verdict and entry-point arms -> after: the same, plus the composed brief naming its findings file on a line the grammar reads back -> not the same: a new arm. Ready to bless. `test_the_dispute_brief_names_the_findings_file_it_was_composed_over` holds it, and it matches LLR-314 and LLR-315.\n\nVERDICT: MEANING rows=7\n\nSITTING: JUDGED kinds=amendment\n"}}}
